}}In "{{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|qid=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}}" {{ill|lt=Engishiki Shinmyocho|Q11064932|qid=Q11064932|lt=Shinmyocho|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}}, there is one enshrined deity. In the same book, it is listed as ``Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine,'' but since this name means ``the shrine of the Amaterasu Mitama deity enshrined in Kijima (place name),'' it was originally a shrine dedicated to ``Amateru Mitama no Kami.'' In addition to Kijima Shrine in {{ill|lt=Yamashiro Province|Q749276|qid=Q749276|1=Yamashiro Province}}, there are also {{ill|lt=Yamato Province|Q907495|qid=Q907495|1=Yamato Province}}, {{ill|lt=Settsu Province|Q864163|qid=Q864163|1=Settsu Province}}, {{ill|lt=Tamba Province|Q954511|qid=Q954511|1=Tanba Province}}, {{ill|lt=Harima Province|Q1047523|qid=Q1047523|1=Harima Province}}, { There are shrines dedicated to Amaterasu Mitama, Amaterasu, and Amaterasu Tamakami in places such as {jalink|Tsushima Country|Q1054861}}, but these are thought to be sun gods with a different deity from {{ill|lt=Amaterasu Omikami|Q455602|qid=Q455602|1=Amaterasu}} (King Ancestor God)<ref>{{ill|lt=Ken Matsumae|Q11529297|qid=Q11529297|1=Takeshi Matsumae}} “A book that helps you understand the mysteries of Japanese mythology” Yamato Shobo, 2007, pp. 42-43. </ref>.Regarding the deity of Amaterasu Mitama-no-Kami of Kijima Shrine, historical sources suggest the theory of Amaterasu no Kuni Teruten Kamei no Mikoto ({{ill|lt=Amenka Mei-no-Mikoto|Q10940411|qid=Q10940411|1=Amenohoakari}}) and Amaterasu Omikami theory.・{{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (National History)}}{{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shinto Dictionary)|1941}} where you can see the theory of divine command. Based on the fact that the current enshrined deity of many of the shrines named Amaterasu Mitama-kami, Amaterasu-kami, and Amaterasu-tama-kami mentioned above is Tenka Mei-no-Mikoto ({{ill|lt=Owari clan|Q11465311|qid=Q11465311|1=Owari clan}} ancestor deity), there is a theory that these deities are especially Hosai-kami of the Owari clan, and in that theory, Kijima Originally, there were people from the Owari clan who worshiped the god Amaterasu Mitama in the area of the shrine, but it is explained that with the arrival and cultivation of the {{ill|lt=Hata clan|Q987290|qid=Q987290|1=Hata clan}}, the local rituals were inherited.<ref>{{ill|lt=Chiaki Mizutani|Q11549157|qid=Q11549157|1=Chiaki Mizutani}} “Mysterious Foreigner Hata Clan (Bunshun Shinsho 734)” Bungeishunju, 2009, pp. 202-203. </ref>. Also, among the Amaterasu deities that can be found in the "Engishiki" divine name book, it is believed that {{ill|lt=Owari clan|Q11465311|qid=Q11465311|1=Owari clan}} and their kin were involved in the rituals. , {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Mizusakuza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine|1=UNKNOWN}}・{{ill|lt=Kagamiza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine|Q38278496|qid=Q38278496|1=Kagamitsukuri Nimasu Amateru Mitama Shrine}}・{{ill|lt=Arayaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine|Q11501968|qid=Q11501968|1=Niiya ni Masu Amateru Mitama Shrine}}, {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Amaterasutama no Mikoto Shrine|1=UNKNOWN}}, {{ill|lt=Tokuza Amaterasu Shrine|Q11604472|qid=Q11604472|1=Iibo Nimasu Amaterasu Shrine}}, {{ill|lt=There is one shrine that is believed to have been worshiped by the Tsushima clan|Q1054861|qid=Q1054861|lt=Tsushima clan|1=Tsushima Province}} and others, and there is one shrine that remains. Rinokijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine・{{ill|lt=Otaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine|Q43594600|qid=Q43594600|1=Osada Nimasu Amateru Mitama Shrine}}・{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Amaterasu Omikami Takaza Shrine|1=UNKNOWN}}・{{jalink |Ise Amaterasu Shrine}} Although it is not possible to confirm the four shrines due to lack of historical materials, Kijima Shrine is the enshrined deity of {{ill|lt=Amadaru Shrine|Q123118284|qid=Q123118284|1=Amateru Shrine}}・Like {{ill|lt=Tenhijin-no-Mikoto|Q133505245|qid=Q133505245|1=Ame-no-hi-no-mitama-no-Mikoto}}, it was a sun god worshiped by the {{ill|lt=Tsushima Province|Q1054861|qid=Q1054861|lt=Tsushima clan|1=Tsushima Province}}. There is a theory that <ref>Katsuki Hojo, "On the enshrinement of Ichikishima Hime no Mikoto at Matsuo Taisha Shrine" (Research Report of the National Museum of Japanese History, Vol. 72, March 1997)</ref>. On the other hand, the direction of the {{ill|lt=three pillar torii|Q3532297|qid=Q3532297|1=Mihashira Torii}} in the grounds of Kijima Shrine is the winter solstice line of Mt. Inari and Mt. Matsuo, and the direction of Mt. Shimeidake and Mt. Atago. (Kyoto City)|Q2975204|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Gods)|1986}} is believed to be related to the summer solstice line of Mt. Atago, and because of the structure in which the sun shines through the Motadasu Pond in the precincts, there is a theory that it is a peninsular sun god (son of the sun) based on the sunlight-sensing type of worship on the Korean Peninsula.{{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Gods)|1986}} In addition, there is a theory that the god is the god of {{ill|lt=Musuhi|Q11279555|qid=Q11279555|lt=Musuhi|1=Musubi}}, who produces grain from the spring water in the shrine grounds, based on the name of the god ``Mimusubi'' {{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shikiuchi Shrine)|1979}}.As such, although the deity of the ancient enshrined deity is unknown, the names of the five deities mentioned above are listed in the ``Kazuno-gun Shrine Detail Book'' of {{ill|lt=1883|Q7816|qid=Q7816|1=1883}} ({{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|qid=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}16), and these have been inherited to the present {{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shikinai Shrine)|1979}}.

=== History ===

=== Founding ===
Founding date is unknown {{Sfn|Excavation report|2002|pp=3-5}}. One theory is that in {{ill|lt=Emperor Suiko|Q298057|qid=Q298057|1=Empress Suiko}}12 ({{ill|lt=604|Q31048|qid=Q31048|1=604}}) {{jalink|Koryuji|Q3076920} }}It is said to have been {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=requested|1=UNKNOWN}} with the founding of Kijima Shrine (Heibonsha)|1979}}{{Sfn|excavation survey report|2002|p=17}}. Historical materials confirm the existence of rituals prior to the article in the first year of {{ill|lt=Taiho (Japan)|Q1346269|qid=Q1346269|lt=Taiho|1=Taihō (era)}} ({{ill|lt=701|Q23496|qid=Q23496|1=701}}).As mentioned above (section ``{{ill|lt=#enshrined deity|UNKNOWN|lt=enshrined deity}}''), there are various theories about the origin of the ritual, but the area around Sagano and Uzumasa was developed by the {{ill|lt=Hata clan|Q987290|qid=Q987290|1=Hata clan}}, an immigrant clan, and {{ill|lt=Koryuji|Q3076920|qid=Q3076920|1=Kōryū-ji}}. Kijima Shrine is also said to be a shrine associated with the Hata clan, as related temples and historical sites such as {{ill|lt=Matsuo Taisha|Q710302|qid=Q710302|1=Matsunoo Taisha}} and {{ill|lt=Jabizuka Tumulus|Q11625508|qid=Q11625508|1=Hebizuka Kofun}} are known.{{Sfn|Kijima Shrine (Heibonsha)|1979}}. It is also assumed that the Seriyo Shrine, which currently sits to the east of the main shrine, is associated with the silkworm farming, weaving, and dyeing techniques introduced by the Hata clan {{Sfn|Seriyo Shrine (Gods)|1986}}. However, even before the arrival of the Hata clan, it is known that there were ruins near Kijima Shrine that represent settlements from around the {{ill|lt=Yayoi period|Q207858|qid=Q207858|1=Yayoi period}}, such as the Izumi Shikibucho ruins.{{Sfn|Excavation report|2002|pp=3-5}}.{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Katsuki Hojo|1=UNKNOWN}} writes about the involvement of the same person named {{ill|lt=Akojidai|Q57538465|qid=Q57538465|1=Akojidai}} (Kotoshiro is a god It is said that it means a symbolic person who entrusts and speaks words), and the god who gives oracles to the world is the moon god and sun god, whose ancestor was {{ill|qid=Q125400|lt=Takamimusubi|UNKNOWN|lt=Takōsanreimyo|1=Takamimusubi}}, and the clan that presides over the rituals. Judging from the response of the person in charge of the rituals, who said that they were a former Kokuzo clan in Iki and Tsushima, based on the island of }} and issuing {{ill|lt=Urabe|Q94724|qid=Q94724|1=Radical 25}}, it is clear that the sun and moon gods of Iki and Tsushima are closely related, and that Kijima Shrine was originally enshrined by the Tsushima clan. It is unknown whether it was worshiped by the {{ill|lt=Owari clan|Q11465311|qid=Q11465311|1=Owari clan}} lineage, but if we consider the above-mentioned correspondence that appears in the February and April periods of the 3rd year of Kenso, Kijima Shrine, which enshrines the sun god, is the {{ill|lt=Tsukiyomi Shrine in Kazuno. (Kyoto City)|Q11516217|qid=Q11516217|lt=Tsukiyomi Shrine|1=Tsukiyomi Shrine (Kyoto)}} cannot be ignored. <ref>Katsuki Hojo, ``On the Enshrinement of Ichikishima Hime no Mikoto at Matsuo Taisha Shrine'' (Research Report of the National Museum of Japanese History, Vol. 72, March 1997).

=== Outline ===
The first look at {{ill|lt=Rokukokushi|Q1151168|qid=Q1151168|lt=Kokushi|1=Rikkokushi}} is "{{ill|lt=Shoku Nihongi|Q611399|qid=Q611399|1=Shoku Nihongi}}" {{ill|lt=Taiho (Japan)|Q1346269|qid=Q1346269|lt=Taiho|1=Taihō (era)}} 1st year ({{ill|lt=701 years|Q23496|qid=Q23496|1=701}}) <ref group="Hara" name="Taiho 1st year">Shoku Nihongi, April 3rd, Taiho 1st year (701) (see {{Harvnb|Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials Collection}}). In Article </ref>, in addition to "Kijima-kami" of {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Yamashikuni|1=UNKNOWN}}{{ill|lt=Kazuno-gun|Q13579879|qid=Q13579879|1=Kadono district}}, {{ill|lt=Tsukiyomi Shrine (Kyoto City)|Q11516217|qid=Q11516217|lt=Tsukiyomijin|1=Tsukiyomi Shrine (Kyoto)}} and {{ill|lt=Mizunushi Shrine (Joyo City)|Q54153262|qid=Q54153262|lt=Kabai God|1=Mizushi Shrine}} and {{ill|lt=Hazukashizatakamibunichi Shrine|Q65278999|qid=Q65278999|lt=Hatsukashi God|1=Hazukashi Shrine}}'s sacred rice appears to have been given to {{ill|lt=Nakatomi clan|Q1198447|qid=Q1198447|1=Nakatomi clan}} from the same year onward.{{Sfn|Kijima Shrine (Heibonsha)|1979}}.“{{ill|lt=Shinsho Kokufusho|Q11502312|qid=Q11502312|1=Shinshō Kyakuchokufushō}}” {{ill|lt=Daido (Japan)|Q1373191|qid=Q1373191|lt=Daido|1=Daidō}} 1st year ({{ill|lt=806 years|Q30021|qid=Q30021|1=806}}) <ref group="Hara">"Shinsho Kakuchofusho" Volume 10 (Shinto rituals and feudal clans) dated 1st year of Daido (806) (see {{Harvnb|Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials Collection}}). According to </ref>, at that time, 9 houses from Yamashiro Province were allocated to ``Koshimakami'' as {{ill|lt=Kobe (Minbe)|Q11589711|qid=Q11589711|lt=Kobe|1=Kanbe (social class)}} {{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shikiuchi Shrine)|1979}}. Continuing with the national history, {{ill|lt=Tean (Japan)|Q1075611|qid=Q1075611|lt=Tenan|1=Ten'an}}3 ({{ill|lt=859|Q23897|qid=Q23897|1=859}}) January <ref group="Hara" In the name="1st year of Jogan"/>, there is a report that {{ill|lt=Shinka|Q11591025|qid=Q11591025|1=Shinkai (divine rank)}} of "Kijima Amaterasu Mitamashin" was promoted to the lower rank of Shogoi, and in September of the same year ({{ill|lt=Jōkan (Japan)|Q1075635|qid=Q1075635|lt=Jōkan|1=Jōgan}}1st year)<ref group="Hara"> "Nihon Sandai Jiroku" September 8, 859, article (see {{Harvnb|Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials Collection}}). </ref>・July, 17th year of Jogan ({{ill|lt=875|Q23816|qid=Q23816|1=875}}) <ref group="Hara"> Article dated July 2, 17th year of Jogan (875) from "Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku" (see {{Harvnb|Collection of Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials}}). </ref>・{{ill|lt=Genkei|Q747451|qid=Q747451|1=Gangyō}} 1st year ({{ill|lt=877|Q23905|qid=Q23905|1=877}}) June 1st year ({{ill|lt=877|Q23905|qid=Q23905|1=877}}) <ref group="Hara"> "Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku" article dated June 14, Genkei 1st year (877) (see {{Harvnb|Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials Collection}}). </ref> includes offerings for praying for rain, August 25, 17th year of the Jogan era <ref group="Hara"> "Nihon Kiryaku" article dated August 25, 875 (see {{Harvnb|Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials Collection}}). </ref> records that a gift was sent for Akiminor, as well as an article dated April 1, 877 in the April 13th year of the Genkei era (refer to {{Harvnb|Collection of Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials}}). The description can also be found in </ref>.{{ill|lt=Extension (era name)|Q1327274|qid=Q1327274|lt=Extension|1=Enchō}} In ``{{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|qid=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}}'' established in 5 years ({{ill|lt=927|Q30470|qid=Q30470|1=927}}) {{ill|lt=Engishiki Shinmeicho|Q11064932|qid=Q11064932|lt=Shinmeicho|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}}, "Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine" is located in Kazuno District, Yamashiro Province. Meishin University It is listed at {{ill|lt=Meishin Taisha|Q9610964|qid=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}} as ``Monthly Ainame Shinnai'' and is also held at the {{ill|lt=Monthly Festival|Q11516161|qid=Q11516161|1=Tsukinami-no-Matsuri}} and {{ill|lt=Ainamesai|Q11581944|qid=Q11581944|1=Ainame Festival}}・In {{ill|lt=Ninamesai|Q11501518|qid=Q11501518|1=Niiname-no-Matsuri}}, it is written that it was kept in {{ill|lt=heihaku|Q11481885|qid=Q11481885|1=Hanpei}}{{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shikiuchi Shrine)|1979}}. The company's name is pronounced as "Amaterasu Mimusuhi" in the Yoshida family book and "Amateru Mimusuhi" in the Kongoji book, but later in the ``{{ill|lt=Shinmyocho kosho|Q11588824|qid=Q11588824|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō Studies}}''・In ``Jingishiryo'' and ``{{ill|lt=Shinja Roku|Q11590708|qid=Q11590708|1=Jinja Kakuroku}}'', it is called ``Amateru Mitama''{{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shikiuchi Shrine)|1979}}. In "Engishiki," Kijima Shrine is also mentioned in the Shijisai Shita Ainosaijo, the Extraordinary Festival Prayer Festival, and the Extraordinary Festival Meishinsaijo."Koryuji Raiyuki" which is {{ill|lt=Engage|Q522046|qid=Q522046|1=Pratītyasamutpāda}} of {{ill|lt=Koryuji|Q3076920|qid=Q3076920|1=Kōryū-ji}} (Koryuji Raiyuki, Kaedeno Oigo, Kadeno Oigo, Kazuno District, Yamashiro Prefecture, established in {{ill|lt=Meio|Q1193841|qid=Q1193841|1=Meiō}} 8 ({{ill|lt=1499|Q6708|qid=Q6708|1=1499}})) <ref name="RAIYUKI">[{{NDLDC|1879799/384}} "Gunshoju Shinkyo Volume 18"] (Naigai Publishing, 1931-1937, National Diet Library Digital Collection) 384-388 frames. </ref>In the Jogan era ({{ill|lt=859|Q23897|qid=Q23897|1=859}} - In {{ill|lt=877|Q23905|qid=Q23905|1=877}}), the monk {{ill|lt=Dosho|Q11367403|qid=Q11367403|1=Dōshō}} performed blessings for rain at the pond of Kijima Meishin by imperial order, and was revered as the god of rain, so he was successively promoted to the divine rank. It is written that he reached the highest rank of Shoichii in {{ill|lt=Nagakyu|Q1193530|qid=Q1193530|1=Chōkyū}}4 ({{ill|lt=1043|Q19348|qid=Q19348|1=1043}}), and it is said that Kijima {{sub| {{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shikiuchi Shrine)|1979}}. In the late {{ill|lt=Heian period|Q193292|qid=Q193292|1=Heian period}} "{{ill|lt=Ryojin Hisho|Q3010584|qid=Q3010584|1=Ryōjin Hishō}}", Kijima Shrine is {{ill|lt=Fushimi Inari Taisha|Q714828|qid=Q714828|1=Fushimi Inari-taisha}} and {{ill|lt=Iwashimizu Hachimangu Shrine|Q710098|qid=Q710098|1=Iwashimizu Hachimangū}}, it is said to be a shrine that was constantly crowded with worshipers. {{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=3-5}}.In {{ill|lt=Jokyu War|Q1372308|qid=Q1372308|1=Jōkyū War}} of {{ill|lt=Jokyu|Q1195428|qid=Q1195428|1=Jōkyū}} 3rd year ({{ill|lt=1221|Q5393|qid=Q5393|1=1221}}) of {{ill|lt=Kamakura period|Q236205|qid=Q236205|1=Kamakura period}}, "{{ill|lt=Azuma Kagami|Q1042324|qid=Q1042324|1=Azuma Kagami}}" <ref group="Hara">Azuma Kagami article dated June 15, 1221. In </ref>, it appears that {{ill|lt=Emperor Gotoba|Q316658|qid=Q316658|lt=Retired Emperor Gotoba|1=Emperor Go-Toba}}'s {{ill|lt=Taneyoshi Miura|Q11356551|qid=Q11356551|1=Miura Taneyoshi}} and his children Tanenren and Kaneyoshi committed suicide at ``Nishiyama Kijima'', but this is believed to be the place {{Sfn|Kijima Shrine (Heibonsha)|1979}}. According to ``{{ill|lt=Jōkyūki|Q11497315|qid=Q11497315|1=Jōkyūki}}'', Taneyoshi was hiding in ``Konojima Shrine'', but when he realized his fate, he committed suicide, and Rojo set the shrine on fire {{Sfn|Kijima Shrine (Heibonsha)|1979}}. Recent excavations within the shrine grounds have revealed traces of land leveling in the early Kamakura period, which appears to have been done during the rebuilding of the shrine building after it was destroyed by fire.{{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=14-16}}

After that, it is not mentioned in literature until around {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|qid=Q184963|1=Edo period}}, and its history is unknown {{Sfn|Excavation report|2002|pp=3-5}}. {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Yasunaga|1=UNKNOWN}}9 ({{ill|lt=1780|Q7716|qid=Q7716|1=1780}})'s ``{{ill|lt=Capital Meisho Zue|Q11643495|qid=Q11643495|1=Miyako meisho zue}}'' depicts the precincts at that time {{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=3-5}}.After {{ill|lt=Meiji Restoration|Q8707|qid=Q8707|1=Meiji Restoration}}, in June {{ill|lt=1873|Q7757|qid=Q7757|1=1873}} ({{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|qid=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}6) {{Sfn|Meiji Shrine Magazine Materials|1912}} ranked as {{ill|lt=Gosha|Q134917316|qid=Q134917316|1=Gō-sha}} in {jalink|Modern Shrine Ranking System|Q712534}}.

In {{ill|lt=1985|Q2431|qid=Q2431|1=1985}} ({{ill|lt=Showa|Q327567|qid=Q327567|1=Shōwa era}}60), the precincts were designated as a designated historic site by Kyoto City <ref name="historic site"/>. In {{ill|lt=2002|Q1987|qid=Q1987|1=2002}} ({{ill|lt=Heisei|Q234510|qid=Q234510|1=Heisei era}}14), an excavation survey was conducted for the first time as a historic site in conjunction with the installation of a fire tank in the northeast corner of the precincts.{{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=3-5}}.

