=== Precincts ===
[[File:Konoshima-jinja honden.JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Main shrine (left) and Seriyo Shrine (right)}}]]
The precincts are located at the eastern end of the low terrace on the right bank of the Omuro River, just south of {{ill|lt=Futagaoka|Q18337635|qid=Q18337635|1=Narabigaoka}} (Futagaoka) and just east of {{ill|lt=Koryuji|Q3076920|qid=Q3076920|1=Kōryū-ji}}. The torii gate is located almost due east of the south gate of Koryuji Temple.{{Sfn|Excavation Report|2002|pp=3-5}}. Kijima Shrine appears to have existed in literature as far back as the {{ill|lt=Taiho (Japan)|Q1346269|qid=Q1346269|lt=Taiho|1=Taihō (era)}} 1st year ({{ill|lt=701|Q23496|qid=Q23496|1=701}}) <ref group="hara" name="Taiho 1st year"/>, making it one of the oldest shrines in Kyoto City. The presence of Motodadasu Pond suggests a connection with the written article on praying for rain, the existence of the Seriyo Shrine suggests a connection with the {{ill|lt=Hata clan|Q987290|qid=Q987290|1=Hata clan}}, and the shrine's grove of giant trees reveals its ancient appearance, making the precincts an important site in the history of Kyoto. It has been designated as a Kyoto City designated historical site because of its location. Kyoto City Designated/Registered Cultural Properties -Historical Sites- > Kijimaza Amaterasu Goryo Shrine (Silkworm Shrine) Precincts] (Kyoto City homepage). </ref><ref name="Explanation board"/>.All of the shrine buildings were rebuilt after the Meiji era {{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=3-5}}. The main shrine is built on a slightly higher ground near the center north of the precincts {{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=3-5}}. To the east of the main shrine is Kokai Shrine (Higashi Honden), and the common name ``Silkworm Shrine'' is derived from this shrine. In front of these is a place of worship.

[[File:Konoshima-jinja mihashira-torii.JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Motonadasu Pond and {{ill|lt=Three Pillar Torii|Q3532297|qid=Q3532297|1=Mihashira Torii}}}}]]
In the northwest corner of the precincts, there is a sacred spring called ``Mototadasu no Ike'', which is now dry, but it is said that it once had an abundance of spring water, and people still visit this spring on the day of the ox in summer. It is believed that soaking the hands and feet in the water is good for various illnesses. According to legend, the shrine complex of Kijima Shrine is called "Motodadasu no Mori" and the shrine spring is called "Motodadasu no Pond", and the forest of {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shimogamo Shrine|1=UNKNOWN}} is called "{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tadasu no Mori|1=UNKNOWN}}, the shrine complex of Kijima Shrine was originally called ``Tadasu no Mori'' {{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|p=17}}.A '''{{ill|lt=Three-Pillared Torii|Q3532297|qid=Q3532297|1=Mihashira Torii}}''' (Mihashira Torii, Three-sided Torii/Triangular Torii) is built inside this Motonadasu Pond. This is an unusual type of {{ill|lt=Torii|Q234731|qid=Q234731|1=Torii}} that has three pillars arranged in a triangle, making it possible to worship the central shrine from three sides.{{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Shikiuchi Shrine)|1979}}, it is counted as one of the "Three Torii Gates of Kyoto"<ref name="Explanation board"/><ref group="Note">"Kyoto Three Torii" is the three pillar torii gate of Kijima Shrine, {{ill|lt=Kyoto Gyoen National Garden|Q843854|qid=Q843854|1=Kyoto Gyoen National Garden}} {{ill|lt=Itsukushima Shrine (Kamigyo Ward, Kyoto City)|Q11410134|qid=Q11410134|lt=Itsukushima Shrine|1=Itsukushima Shrine (Kyoto)}}'s Karahafu torii, {{ill|lt=Kitano Tenmangu|Q662176|qid=Q662176|1=Kitano Tenmangū}} Three stone torii gates at Tomoji Shrine. </ref>. The central shrine is made of pebbles piled up in a conical shape and a gohei placed in the center to serve as {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Yorishiro|1=UNKNOWN}}{{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=3-5}}. The origin of this torii is not clear, but it is a sacred place for the Hata clan, {{ill|lt=Futagaoka|Q18337635|qid=Q18337635|1=Narabigaoka}}, Mt. Matsuo ({{ill|lt=Matsuo Taisha|Q710302|qid=Q710302|1=Matsunoo Taisha}} Shintaiyama), There is a theory that it represents the direction of distant worship of Mt. Inari ({{ill|lt=Fushimi Inari Taisha|Q714828|qid=Q714828|1=Fushimi Inari-taisha}} Shintaiyama){{Sfn|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Gods)|1986}}<ref group="Note"> Regarding the three-pillar torii, {{ill|lt=Christianity|Q5043|qid=Q5043|1=Christianity}}{{jalink|Keikyo|Q13417} of {{ill|lt=Nestorian|Q168414|qid=Q168414|1=Nestorianism}} 194}}, and there was a popular theory that Keikyo was established by the Hata clan in the future, but this has been denied as Keikyo was originally imported to China in 635 and officially recognized in 638, which was quite a while after the time when the Hata clan arrived. {{Harv|Kijimaza Amaterasu Mitama Shrine (Gods)|1986}}. </ref>. The current torii was rebuilt in {{ill|lt=Tenpo|Q1369045|qid=Q1369045|1=Tenpō}}2 years ({{ill|lt=1831|Q7579|qid=Q7579|1=1831}}), but {{Sfn|excavation survey report|2002|pp=3-5}} (according to the shrine's biography, it was {{ill|lt=Kyoho|Q1145048|qid=Q1145048|1=Kyōhō}} years ({{ill|lt=1716|Q6892|qid=Q6892|1=1716}} - {{ill|lt=1735|Q6984|qid=Q6984|1=1735}}) restoration {{Sfn|Kijima Shrine (Heibonsha)|1979}}), {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Yasunaga|1=UNKNOWN}}9 ({{ill|lt=1780|Q7716|qid=Q7716|1=1780}}) ``{{ill|lt=Capital Meisho Zue|Q11643495|qid=Q11643495|1=Miyako meisho zue}}'' depicts the same three-pillar torii gate as seen today, along with abundant spring water.{{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=3-5}}.Excavation research in {{ill|lt=2002|Q1987|qid=Q1987|1=2002}} ({{ill|lt=Heisei|Q234510|qid=Q234510|1=Heisei era}}14) revealed that there used to be springs in various places on the east side of the main shrine, and it is believed that Motonadasu Pond was one of these springs that was deified.{{Sfn|Excavation Report|2002|pp=14-16}} During this excavation, remains of a stone pavement associated with a spring dating back to at least the middle of the Heian period were unearthed.{{Sfn|Excavation Survey Report|2002|pp=14-16}}

* Main shrine - Rebuilt in the {{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|qid=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}} era.
* Place of worship - Rebuilt during the Meiji era.
* Worship Hall - Rebuilt during the Meiji era.
* Motadasu Pond
